{"headline":"Your bench press is surging forward — keep this momentum going strong.","summary":"This was a productive week with clear progress where it matters most. Your chest strength index jumped from 106 to 112, the biggest single-period gain across all muscle groups, which is excellent news for your 100 kg bench press goal. Triceps also ticked up nicely from 105 to 108, providing solid support for that goal. The heavy chest focus with 41% of total volume dedicated to it shows purposeful, goal-aligned training. The main area to watch is back, which dipped from 101 to 96, suggesting that muscle group needs a little more attention. Overall, this was a strong and encouraging period — you are moving in the right direction.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"on_track","notes":"Chest is the standout performer this period, climbing from a strength index of 106 to 112 — a meaningful 5.7% jump that directly supports the 100 kg bench press goal. Triceps also progressed from 105 to 108, which is important since triceps are a key driver of bench press lockout strength. Shoulders held steady at 104, which is fine. Biceps and legs remained flat at 100 and 102 respectively, but those are not priority movers for your goal. The one concern is back, which regressed from 101 to 96. Back strength, particularly the lats and upper back, plays a supporting role in bench press stability and scapular retraction, so this dip is worth monitoring and addressing."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"back","severity":"mild","observation":"The back received only 4 sets and 19% of volume this period, and its strength index fell from 101 to 96. Given that a strong, stable back is essential for safe and efficient bench pressing, this mild regression and relatively low volume allocation should be corrected before it becomes a limiting factor."},{"muscleGroup":"biceps","severity":"mild","observation":"Biceps received only 2 sets and 2.7% of total volume, the lowest of any muscle group tracked. While biceps are not directly critical for bench pressing, they support elbow health and overall pulling balance. A small increase in direct bicep work would help maintain structural balance."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Continue prioritizing chest volume and intensity — 9 sets per period is a solid investment toward your 100 kg bench press goal. Aim to push slightly heavier loads or add a rep where possible to keep the strength index climbing.","Add 2 to 3 extra sets of back work next period, such as barbell rows or cable rows, to reverse the dip from 101 to 96. A strong upper back directly improves bench press stability and reduces injury risk.","Give your triceps a focused stimulus with at least one dedicated triceps exercise like close-grip bench press or skull crushers, building on the positive trend from 105 to 108 this period.","Bring bicep training up to at least 3 to 4 sets next period. This small adjustment will maintain pushing and pulling balance and protect your elbow joints as bench loads continue to increase."],"contextSummary":"Chest strength index made a strong jump to 112 and triceps rose to 108, signaling great bench press progress; back regressed to 96 and needs extra volume attention next period."}